WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.140
We have simplified resistor networks and modeled real cells.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.319
Now we need a method that still works when several sources and branches

00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:09.680
prevent

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.580
easy series or parallel reduction.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.540
Kirchhoff's first law is the junction rule.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.680
In a steady circuit, charge does not keep accumulating at a junction, so total

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.800
current entering equals total current leaving.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.580
This is charge conservation in action.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.420
Suppose five amperes enters a junction, while two amperes leaves along one

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:29.680
path.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.780
The remaining outgoing path carries three amperes.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.140
No current has been consumed at the node.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.740
Before solving an unfamiliar circuit, choose a current arrow for each branch.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.840
You are allowed to guess its direction.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.220
The arrow defines a positive reference; it does not guarantee the physical

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:46.620
result.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.040
If an equation returns minus one ampere, actual current is one ampere opposite

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:52.520
your arrow.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.000
Keep that negative sign when checking the original equations.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.140
Changing arrows halfway through without rewriting the equations creates

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:00.640
confusion.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.360
We assume steady direct current and ideal connecting wires in this class.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.820
For a connected circuit with several nodes, some junction equations repeat the

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:10.520
same information.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.940
Usually one fewer independent junction equation than the number of nodes is

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:15.260
sufficient.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.957
Begin by tracing uninterrupted wires into nodes, then write the current

00:01:19.957 --> 00:01:20.320
balance

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.620
at each independent junction.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.080
A complicated drawing becomes manageable when every current has a clear

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:26.800
reference direction.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.160
Kirchhoff's second law is the loop rule.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.920
Walk around a closed path and return to the same point.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.160
In our steady circuit model, the total signed change in electric potential is

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:36.480
zero.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.760
Sources supply energy per charge, and resistors transfer it into internal

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.160
energy.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.360
Choose a direction to walk around a loop; clockwise or counterclockwise both

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:45.660
work.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.460
This walking direction is separate from the current arrows.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.260
Across a resistor, walking with its reference current gives minus I R, a

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:53.840
potential drop.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.100
Walking against the reference arrow gives plus I R.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.140
Across an ideal source, walking from its negative terminal to its positive

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.480
terminal gives plus E.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.680
Walking from positive to negative gives minus E.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.600
Internal resistance is another resistor and follows the same resistor rule.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.452
Try one source of twelve volts, internal resistance one ohm and load five

00:02:14.452 --> 00:02:14.720
ohms.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.294
Walking through the source from negative to positive, then with current

00:02:18.294 --> 00:02:18.560
through

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.440
both resistances, gives twelve minus I times one minus I times five equals

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:23.740
zero.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.500
Current is two amperes.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.020
Reverse your entire walk and every sign reverses; the current solution remains

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:30.580
unchanged.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.880
That is a useful consistency check.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.740
This zero sum form assumes no changing magnetic flux supplies an unaccounted

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.600
induced EMF.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.220
Later, induction will extend the model.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.300
For now, mark polarities, draw current arrows and write each potential change

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.620
in the order you encounter it.

00:02:47.832 --> 00:02:50.752
Let's set up an original worked problem carefully.

00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:55.792
There are two nodes, A at the top and B at the bottom, with three branches

00:02:55.792 --> 00:02:56.432
between them.

00:02:56.992 --> 00:03:01.046
The left branch has a twelve volt ideal source and a two ohm series

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:01.712
resistance.

00:03:02.312 --> 00:03:06.712
The right branch has a six volt ideal source and a two ohm series resistance.

00:03:07.412 --> 00:03:09.852
Both sources have their positive terminal toward A.

00:03:10.272 --> 00:03:12.492
The middle branch is a four ohm load.

00:03:13.252 --> 00:03:17.832
Choose I one upward from B to A in the left branch, I two upward in the right

00:03:17.832 --> 00:03:20.332
branch, and I three downward through the load.

00:03:20.852 --> 00:03:24.812
At node A, write I one plus I two equals I three.

00:03:24.812 --> 00:03:28.665
Now walk from B upward through the left source branch and downward through the

00:03:28.665 --> 00:03:28.912
load.

00:03:29.472 --> 00:03:33.872
The source raises potential by twelve, its series resistor lowers it by two I

00:03:33.872 --> 00:03:36.852
one, and the load lowers it by four I three.

00:03:36.852 --> 00:03:37.632
Their sum is zero.

00:03:38.172 --> 00:03:42.692
For the right loop, the same walk gives six minus two I two minus four I three

00:03:42.692 --> 00:03:43.452
equals zero.

00:03:43.792 --> 00:03:47.212
We have three unknown branch currents and three independent equations.

00:03:47.812 --> 00:03:51.592
The loop across the two source branches would add no new information; it

00:03:51.592 --> 00:03:53.872
follows by subtracting these two loop equations.

00:03:54.832 --> 00:03:59.072
Pause before solving and check the picture: source polarities, resistor values

00:03:59.212 --> 00:04:01.932
and chosen current arrows must match every term.

00:04:02.352 --> 00:04:06.332
The six volt branch may turn out to absorb energy rather than supply it.

00:04:06.332 --> 00:04:06.600
Our signed equations will tell us.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.520
Let V be the potential of A relative to B.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.980
The load current is V divided by four.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.780
The left source current is twelve minus V divided by two, and the right source

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.380
current is six minus V divided by two.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.040
Substituting into the junction equation gives nine minus V equals V over four.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.060
Multiply by four: thirty six minus four V equals V.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.740
Therefore V is seven point two volts.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:36.680
I one becomes two point four amperes, I two becomes minus zero point six

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.540
amperes, and I three becomes one point eight amperes.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.780
The negative result is useful information: actual current in the six volt

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.300
branch travels from A to B, opposite our upward reference arrow.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.960
Current enters that source's positive terminal, so the ideal six volt source

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:53.720
absorbs energy.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:58.198
Check the junction: two point four minus zero point six equals one point

00:04:58.198 --> 00:04:58.500
eight.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.660
Check the left loop: twelve minus four point eight minus seven point two is

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:02.900
zero.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.960
Check the right: six plus one point two minus seven point two is zero.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:08.700
Now verify power.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.680
The twelve volt source supplies twenty eight point eight watts.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:14.680
The six volt source absorbs three point six watts.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.020
Resistor heating totals twenty five point two watts: eleven point five two in

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.880
the left branch, zero point seven two in the right, and twelve point nine six

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:23.440
in the load.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.280
Absorption plus heating equals the supplied power.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.448
A negative current never makes resistor heating negative; use I squared R.

00:05:29.448 --> 00:05:33.368
Let's finish with a routine you can carry into a JEE problem.

00:05:34.328 --> 00:05:36.488
First label nodes by tracing ideal wires.

00:05:37.568 --> 00:05:41.608
Next mark every source polarity and choose one reference current per branch.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:45.608
Write enough independent junction and loop equations for the unknowns.

00:05:46.508 --> 00:05:50.088
Solve the equations, interpret signs, and check the original circuit.

00:05:50.608 --> 00:05:55.188
A branch containing a source cannot generally be merged with another branch by

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:57.268
using only the parallel resistor formula.

00:05:57.828 --> 00:06:01.628
Sources maintain their own potential rises and can oppose one another.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:05.488
Include each source and each internal resistance explicitly.

00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:11.388
For an ideal ammeter, resistance is zero; placing it between two nodes can

00:06:11.388 --> 00:06:13.528
change the circuit by joining their potentials.

00:06:14.348 --> 00:06:18.310
For an ideal voltmeter, resistance is infinite and no current flows through

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:18.468
its

00:06:18.468 --> 00:06:18.768
branch.

00:06:19.468 --> 00:06:23.048
A voltmeter reading of zero does not automatically mean there is no current

00:06:23.048 --> 00:06:23.528
elsewhere.

00:06:24.188 --> 00:06:26.368
Keep meter connections in the diagram while reasoning.

00:06:26.368 --> 00:06:30.788
If you get a negative branch current, retain it in the equations and describe

00:06:30.788 --> 00:06:31.828
the actual direction.

00:06:32.888 --> 00:06:36.488
Check resistor heating with current squared times resistance, and determine

00:06:36.488 --> 00:06:40.148
source power from whether current leaves or enters the positive terminal.

00:06:41.128 --> 00:06:44.508
More elaborate circuits use the same method with more independent equations.

00:06:45.388 --> 00:06:49.068
Bridges will be our next application, where equal potentials can make a

00:06:49.068 --> 00:06:50.428
connecting branch carry zero current.

00:06:50.728 --> 00:06:54.648
For today's class, the important skill is not guessing which source wins.

00:06:54.928 --> 00:06:59.328
It is translating a clearly labeled circuit into consistent signed equations

00:06:59.328 --> 00:07:01.548
and letting those equations answer the question.
